 ### Parameters
-*   $\theta$: Scene parameter. Assumed scalar $\theta \in \mathbb{R}$ for simplicity, generalizable to $\mathbb{R}^m$ [S1]. Derivatives are evaluated at $\theta = 0$ [S1].
-*   $K$: Index of the volume boundary vertex. Must satisfy $0 < K < N$ [S1].
+*   **$\theta$:** Shape parameter. Derivatives evaluated at $\theta=0$ [S2].
+*   **Reference Configuration $\mathcal{B}$:** Typically $\mathcal{M}(\theta_0)$ [S3, unofficial].
 
 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Multiple Boundary Vertices:** Paths with two or more vertices constrained on the boundary have zero measure and make no contribution to the first-order derivatives [S1].
-*   **Visibility Boundaries:** The derivation assumes no reflective/refractive or volume shadow boundaries (Assumption S.1) to ensure material measurement contribution is continuous in the interior of $B_V$, required for the divergence theorem [S1]. If violated, the velocity of visibility boundaries is nontrivial to compute without tetrahedralization [S1].
-*   **Interior Mapping:** Previously, tetrahedral meshes were used to interpolate the mapping $X(\cdot, \theta)$ into the interior, introducing high computational overhead due to frequent tetrahedralizations and point-in-tetrahedron lookups [S1]. This method avoids that [S1].
+*   **Visibility Discontinuities:** If Assumption S.1 is violated (refractive/reflective boundaries or $\theta$-dependent shadows), the velocity of visibility boundaries is nontrivial to compute without tetrahedralization [S2].
+*   **Multiple Boundary Vertices:** Paths with two or more vertices on the boundary have zero measure and contribute nothing to the first-order derivative [S2].
+*   **Computational Cost:** Gradient computation for a single image takes seconds to tens of seconds, making it impractical for neural network training [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Tetrahedralization:** Previous methods required tetrahedralization to interpolate mapping $X$ into the volume interior. This method avoids that overhead [S2].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
-### Unidirectional Estimators
-Monte Carlo estimators for the boundary path integral using unidirectional path tracing. Chosen when the scene geometry allows simple visibility checks. [Link to specific estimator article if available, otherwise link to Parent].
+### Unidirectional Estimator
+Samples paths from camera to light. Simple to implement but high variance in participating media. [Link: [Reparameterization Estimators](6499-reparameterization-estimators.md)]
 
-### Bidirectional Estimators
-Monte Carlo estimators using bidirectional path tracing for the boundary path integral. Chosen for complex light transport involving caustics or deep shadows. [Link to specific estimator article if available, otherwise link to Parent].
+### Bidirectional Estimator
+Samples paths from camera and light, connecting them. Better for complex lighting but more complex to implement with boundary terms. [Link: [Reparameterization Estimators](6499-reparameterization-estimators.md)]
 
 ### Multiple Importance Sampling (MIS)
-MIS variants for combining unidirectional and bidirectional boundary estimators. Chosen to reduce variance in scenes with mixed lighting conditions. [Link to specific estimator article if available, otherwise link to Parent].
+Combines unidirectional and bidirectional estimates to reduce variance. Requires careful weighting of boundary terms. [Link: [Reparameterization Estimators](6499-reparameterization-estimators.md)]
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the Monte Carlo estimator used (unidirectional, bidirectional, MIS). Avoids the $O(N)$ interior mapping evaluations per vertex, reducing overhead compared to tetrahedralization-based methods [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the reference configuration and the mapping $X$. Does not require tetrahedral meshes for the interior [S1].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Validated against finite-difference gradients and compared against Zhang et al.'s Path-Space Differentiable Rendering (PSDR) and Differential Theory of Radiative Transfer (DTRT) [S1]. Inverse rendering compared against Nimier-David et al.'s voxel-based approach [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Eliminates the need for expensive interior mapping evaluations and tetrahedralizations [S1]. Enables the use of sophisticated Monte Carlo estimators beyond unidirectional path tracing [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Assumes no refractive/reflective boundaries or $\theta$-dependent visibility boundaries (Assumption S.1) [S1]. Cannot handle visibility discontinuities without significant modification [S1].
-*   **When to Use:** Differentiable volume rendering of translucent objects with respect to shape parameters, where objects have null BSDFs and no $\theta$-dependent shadows [S1].
-*   **When Not to Use:** Scenes with refractive/reflective boundaries or complex visibility changes dependent on $\theta$ [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** High. Gradient computation for a single image takes seconds to tens of seconds [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on path storage. Avoids storing tetrahedral meshes, reducing overhead compared to previous methods [S2].
+*   **Accuracy:** Unbiased [S1, unofficial]. Validated against finite-difference gradients [S2].
+*   **Strengths:** Does not require explicit silhouette edge finding [S1, unofficial]. Avoids expensive tetrahedralizations [S2]. Handles shape derivatives efficiently for translucent objects [S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Slow for iterative optimization (e.g., neural networks) [S1, unofficial]. Assumes no refractive/reflective boundaries or $\theta$-dependent shadows [S2].
+*   **When to Use:** Inverse rendering of translucent objects where shape optimization is needed and computational time per iteration is not critical.
+*   **When Not to Use:** Real-time differentiable rendering, scenes with complex refractive interfaces, or when training deep neural networks requiring many gradient steps.
 
 ## Background
-The Boundary Path Integral Formulation was introduced by Zhang et al. in 2020 [S1][S2, unofficial]. It is part of the differential path integral framework that formulates both interior and boundary components as full path integrals [S2, unofficial]. It builds on the reparameterization technique using a reference configuration independent of $\theta$ [S2, unofficial]. The method was developed to address the computational overhead of tetrahedralization-based interior mapping in previous works like Zhang et al. 2021 (PSDR) [S1]. It is used in differentiable rendering and inverse rendering of translucent objects [S1].
+The Boundary Path Integral Formulation was introduced by Zhang et al. in 2020 [S1, S3, unofficial]. It extends the differential path integral framework to handle volume rendering with respect to shape parameters [S2]. Prior methods used tetrahedral meshes to interpolate deformation mappings into the volume interior, which was computationally expensive [S2]. This method applies the divergence theorem to convert interior volume integrals into boundary area integrals, eliminating the need for interior mapping evaluations [S2]. It is part of the broader class of reparameterization estimators for differentiable rendering [S1, unofficial].
 
